# Kedge web — environment. Copy to .env.local to override; a fresh clone boots
# with no file at all, because the defaults below are also compiled into the app
# (lib/config.ts). Committed as documentation, mirroring api/.env.example.

# --- API base URL (SPEC §4 auth handshake) ----------------------------------
# The web app reaches the Kedge API from two places, so there are two knobs.
# Changing deployment shape is always an env change, never a code change.
#
#   API_URL              server-side only. The BFF route handlers and Server
#                        Components forward the browser's cookies to this
#                        address to read session state. Never shipped to the
#                        browser, so it may be an internal host. Server-side
#                        fetch needs an absolute URL — do not leave this blank.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  inlined into the browser bundle. Client components call
#                        it directly for the auth mutations (csrf-cookie,
#                        register, login, logout). Set to '' to make those calls
#                        relative/same-origin.
#
# Topologies (mirror api/.env.example):
#
# 1. Dev two-port (default) — web :3000, api :8000
#      API_URL=http://localhost:8000
#      NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
#
# 2. SaaS split-domain — web app.kedge.review, api api.kedge.review
#      API_URL=https://api.kedge.review
#      NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.kedge.review
#
# 3. Self-host single-origin — web + api behind one reverse proxy that routes
#    /api and /sanctum (plus /register /login /logout) to the API. Browser
#    calls go same-origin; the server still needs an absolute address:
#      API_URL=http://api:8000            # internal address the web server dials
#      NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=               # empty → relative, same-origin
#
API_URL=http://localhost:8000
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# --- Text projection service (SPEC §5.4) ------------------------------------
# The internal endpoint at /internal/projection turns normalized content into a
# document's plain-text anchor substrate. It is called only by the API's import
# queue job and must NEVER be reachable from the public internet. In the Next
# app router every route is publicly routable, so the guard is a shared secret
# enforced in the route handler (not a proxy rule): a request without the
# matching `x-projection-secret` header gets a 404.
#
# Set this to the SAME value as the API's PROJECTION_SHARED_SECRET. REQUIRED in
# production — if it is unset there, the endpoint fails closed (404s every
# request) rather than trusting a default. In dev it defaults to a well-known
# value the API also defaults to, so `npm run dev` projects with no config.
PROJECTION_SHARED_SECRET=dev-projection-secret

# --- Re-anchor service (SPEC §8.3) ------------------------------------------
# The internal endpoint at /internal/reanchor is called by the API's manual
# re-sync job after it lands a target version. It uses the same header/guard
# pattern as projection. Set this to the SAME value as the API's
# REANCHOR_SHARED_SECRET. If unset, it falls back to PROJECTION_SHARED_SECRET so
# local dev and existing deployments can share one internal secret.
REANCHOR_SHARED_SECRET=dev-projection-secret

# --- Diagram render cache (SPEC §6.2) ---------------------------------------
# The imported-document diagram component asks the API to render + cache each
# Kroki diagram (server-side, during render), then embeds the cached SVG's
# /storage URL as a plain <img>. The web reaches the API at API_URL above; this
# secret authenticates that internal web→api call (the mirror of the projection
# secret, roles swapped).
#
# Set this to the SAME value as the API's DIAGRAM_SHARED_SECRET. In dev it
# defaults to a well-known value the API also defaults to, so `npm run dev`
# renders diagrams with no config; set a real secret on both sides in production.
DIAGRAM_SHARED_SECRET=dev-diagram-secret
